    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old postmenopausal woman presents for a routine health maintenance visit. She has no history of fractures. A dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA) scan is performed, revealing a T-score of -2.8 at the femoral neck. According to the World Health Organization (WHO) diagnostic criteria, how should this result be classified, and what is the most appropriate next step?', '["Osteoporosis (T-score <= -2.5); initiate pharmacologic therapy with a first-line bisphosphonate plus calcium and vitamin D supplementation", "Indeterminate result; the Z-score, not the T-score, is the standard diagnostic metric for postmenopausal women", "Osteopenia (T-score between -1.0 and -2.5); calculate the FRAX score to determine 10-year fracture probability and guide the treatment decision", "Normal bone density (T-score >= -1.0); reassure the patient and repeat DEXA in 15 years", "Severe osteoporosis; immediately start teriparatide as the only acceptable first-line agent"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The WHO classifies bone mineral density (BMD) by DEXA T-scores: **Normal >= -1.0**, **Osteopenia -1.0 to -2.5**, **Osteoporosis <= -2.5**. The T-score compares a patient''s BMD to a healthy 30-year-old reference (peak bone mass). For postmenopausal women, the T-score (not Z-score) is the standard. Z-scores are used for premenopausal women, men <50, and children. For osteopenia, the **FRAX tool** (Fracture Risk Assessment Tool) should be used to calculate 10-year major osteoporotic and hip fracture probability to guide treatment decisions. Pharmacotherapy is indicated if FRAX shows >=20% major osteoporotic fracture risk or >=3% hip fracture risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; USPSTF 2018; NOF Guidelines)'),
