    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old postmenopausal female presents for a routine health maintenance visit. She has no history of fractures. A dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA) scan is performed, revealing a T-score of -2.8 at the femoral neck. According to the World Health Organization (WHO) diagnostic criteria, how should this result be classified, and what is the most appropriate next step?', '["Normal bone density (T-score >= -1.0); reassure the patient and repeat DEXA in 15 years", "Osteoporosis (T-score <= -2.5); initiate pharmacologic therapy with a first-line bisphosphonate plus calcium and vitamin D supplementation", "Osteopenia (T-score between -1.0 and -2.5); calculate the FRAX score to determine 10-year fracture probability and guide the treatment decision", "Severe osteoporosis; immediately start teriparatide as the only acceptable first-line agent", "Indeterminate result; the Z-score, not the T-score, is the standard diagnostic metric for postmenopausal women"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The WHO classifies bone mineral density (BMD) by DEXA T-scores: **Normal >= -1.0**, **Osteopenia -1.0 to -2.5**, **Osteoporosis <= -2.5**. The T-score compares a patient''s BMD to a healthy 30-year-old reference (peak bone mass). For postmenopausal women, the T-score (not Z-score) is the standard. Z-scores are used for premenopausal women, men <50, and children. For osteopenia, the **FRAX tool** (Fracture Risk Assessment Tool) should be used to calculate 10-year major osteoporotic and hip fracture probability to guide treatment decisions. Pharmacotherapy is indicated if FRAX shows >=20% major osteoporotic fracture risk or >=3% hip fracture risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; USPSTF 2018; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old postmenopausal female presents for a routine health maintenance visit. She has no history of fractures. A dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA) scan is performed, revealing a T-score of -2.9 at the femoral neck. According to the World Health Organization (WHO) diagnostic criteria, how should this result be classified, and what is the most appropriate next step?', '["Osteopenia (T-score between -1.0 and -2.5); calculate the FRAX score to determine 10-year fracture probability and guide the treatment decision", "Indeterminate result; the Z-score, not the T-score, is the standard diagnostic metric for postmenopausal women", "Normal bone density (T-score >= -1.0); reassure the patient and repeat DEXA in 15 years", "Osteoporosis (T-score <= -2.5); initiate pharmacologic therapy with a first-line bisphosphonate plus calcium and vitamin D supplementation", "Severe osteoporosis; immediately start teriparatide as the only acceptable first-line agent"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The WHO classifies bone mineral density (BMD) by DEXA T-scores: **Normal >= -1.0**, **Osteopenia -1.0 to -2.5**, **Osteoporosis <= -2.5**. The T-score compares a patient''s BMD to a healthy 30-year-old reference (peak bone mass). For postmenopausal women, the T-score (not Z-score) is the standard. Z-scores are used for premenopausal women, men <50, and children. For osteopenia, the **FRAX tool** (Fracture Risk Assessment Tool) should be used to calculate 10-year major osteoporotic and hip fracture probability to guide treatment decisions. Pharmacotherapy is indicated if FRAX shows >=20% major osteoporotic fracture risk or >=3% hip fracture risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; USPSTF 2018; NOF Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old woman presents for an annual wellness visit. She has no personal history of fractures, no chronic glucocorticoid use, and no secondary causes of osteoporosis. She asks whether she should be screened for osteoporosis. According to the United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding DEXA screening for this patient?', '["Screen only women with a BMI > 30, as obesity is the strongest risk factor for osteoporosis", "Screen all women with annual DEXA scans beginning at age 40 to establish a longitudinal bone density trend", "Screen all women starting at age 50, regardless of risk factors, because menopause universally causes osteoporosis", "Screen all women aged >= 65 years with DEXA; for women younger than 65, screen only those with clinical risk factors (e.g., low body weight, parental hip fracture history, smoking, excessive alcohol) that result in a comparable fracture risk", "DEXA screening is not recommended for any women until they sustain their first fragility fracture"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **USPSTF recommends DEXA screening for all women aged >= 65 years**. For **postmenopausal women younger than 65**, screening is recommended only for those whose clinical risk profile (assessed using tools like FRAX) indicates a fracture risk equivalent to that of a 65-year-old white woman with no additional risk factors (approximately a 9.3% 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk). Key clinical risk factors include low body weight (<127 lbs / 57.7 kg), parental history of hip fracture, current smoking, and excessive alcohol use. The USPSTF found insufficient evidence to recommend screening in men. Annual screening is not recommended due to radiation exposure and cost. (Ref: USPSTF 2018 Recommendation; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old female presents for an annual wellness visit. She has no personal history of fractures, no chronic glucocorticoid use, and no secondary causes of osteoporosis. She asks whether she should be screened for osteoporosis. According to the United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding DEXA screening for this patient?', '["Screen all women with annual DEXA scans beginning at age 40 to establish a longitudinal bone density trend", "Screen all women aged >= 65 years with DEXA; for women younger than 65, screen only those with clinical risk factors (e.g., low body weight, parental hip fracture history, smoking, excessive alcohol) that result in a comparable fracture risk", "Screen only women with a BMI > 30, as obesity is the strongest risk factor for osteoporosis", "Screen all women starting at age 50, regardless of risk factors, because menopause universally causes osteoporosis", "DEXA screening is not recommended for any women until they sustain their first fragility fracture"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **USPSTF recommends DEXA screening for all women aged >= 65 years**. For **postmenopausal women younger than 65**, screening is recommended only for those whose clinical risk profile (assessed using tools like FRAX) indicates a fracture risk equivalent to that of a 65-year-old white woman with no additional risk factors (approximately a 9.3% 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk). Key clinical risk factors include low body weight (<127 lbs / 57.7 kg), parental history of hip fracture, current smoking, and excessive alcohol use. The USPSTF found insufficient evidence to recommend screening in men. Annual screening is not recommended due to radiation exposure and cost. (Ref: USPSTF 2018 Recommendation; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
