    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female presents for an annual wellness visit. She has no personal history of fractures, no chronic glucocorticoid use, and no secondary causes of osteoporosis. She asks whether she should be screened for osteoporosis. According to the United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding DEXA screening for this patient?', '["DEXA screening is not recommended for any women until they sustain their first fragility fracture", "Screen all women with annual DEXA scans beginning at age 40 to establish a longitudinal bone density trend", "Screen all women starting at age 50, regardless of risk factors, because menopause universally causes osteoporosis", "Screen only women with a BMI > 30, as obesity is the strongest risk factor for osteoporosis", "Screen all women aged >= 65 years with DEXA; for women younger than 65, screen only those with clinical risk factors (e.g., low body weight, parental hip fracture history, smoking, excessive alcohol) that result in a comparable fracture risk"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **USPSTF recommends DEXA screening for all women aged >= 65 years**. For **postmenopausal women younger than 65**, screening is recommended only for those whose clinical risk profile (assessed using tools like FRAX) indicates a fracture risk equivalent to that of a 65-year-old white woman with no additional risk factors (approximately a 9.3% 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk). Key clinical risk factors include low body weight (<127 lbs / 57.7 kg), parental history of hip fracture, current smoking, and excessive alcohol use. The USPSTF found insufficient evidence to recommend screening in men. Annual screening is not recommended due to radiation exposure and cost. (Ref: USPSTF 2018 Recommendation; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old woman presents for an annual wellness visit. She has no personal history of fractures, no chronic glucocorticoid use, and no secondary causes of osteoporosis. She asks whether she should be screened for osteoporosis. According to the United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding DEXA screening for this patient?', '["DEXA screening is not recommended for any women until they sustain their first fragility fracture", "Screen only women with a BMI > 30, as obesity is the strongest risk factor for osteoporosis", "Screen all women starting at age 50, regardless of risk factors, because menopause universally causes osteoporosis", "Screen all women with annual DEXA scans beginning at age 40 to establish a longitudinal bone density trend", "Screen all women aged >= 65 years with DEXA; for women younger than 65, screen only those with clinical risk factors (e.g., low body weight, parental hip fracture history, smoking, excessive alcohol) that result in a comparable fracture risk"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **USPSTF recommends DEXA screening for all women aged >= 65 years**. For **postmenopausal women younger than 65**, screening is recommended only for those whose clinical risk profile (assessed using tools like FRAX) indicates a fracture risk equivalent to that of a 65-year-old white woman with no additional risk factors (approximately a 9.3% 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk). Key clinical risk factors include low body weight (<127 lbs / 57.7 kg), parental history of hip fracture, current smoking, and excessive alcohol use. The USPSTF found insufficient evidence to recommend screening in men. Annual screening is not recommended due to radiation exposure and cost. (Ref: USPSTF 2018 Recommendation; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female presents for an annual wellness visit. She has no personal history of fractures, no chronic glucocorticoid use, and no secondary causes of osteoporosis. She asks whether she should be screened for osteoporosis. According to the United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) guidelines, which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding DEXA screening for this patient?', '["Screen all women starting at age 50, regardless of risk factors, because menopause universally causes osteoporosis", "Screen only women with a BMI > 30, as obesity is the strongest risk factor for osteoporosis", "Screen all women with annual DEXA scans beginning at age 40 to establish a longitudinal bone density trend", "Screen all women aged >= 65 years with DEXA; for women younger than 65, screen only those with clinical risk factors (e.g., low body weight, parental hip fracture history, smoking, excessive alcohol) that result in a comparable fracture risk", "DEXA screening is not recommended for any women until they sustain their first fragility fracture"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **USPSTF recommends DEXA screening for all women aged >= 65 years**. For **postmenopausal women younger than 65**, screening is recommended only for those whose clinical risk profile (assessed using tools like FRAX) indicates a fracture risk equivalent to that of a 65-year-old white woman with no additional risk factors (approximately a 9.3% 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk). Key clinical risk factors include low body weight (<127 lbs / 57.7 kg), parental history of hip fracture, current smoking, and excessive alcohol use. The USPSTF found insufficient evidence to recommend screening in men. Annual screening is not recommended due to radiation exposure and cost. (Ref: USPSTF 2018 Recommendation; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 43-year-old premenopausal woman is referred for bone density testing after sustaining a low-trauma wrist fracture. Her DEXA scan reveals a femoral neck T-score of -2.8 and a Z-score of -2.4. Which of the following is the most accurate interpretation and the most appropriate clinical reasoning regarding the choice of reference metric?', '["Both T-scores and Z-scores are obsolete; quantitative CT (QCT) has replaced DEXA as the gold standard for diagnosing osteoporosis", "The T-score of -2.8 confirms osteoporosis regardless of patient age; initiate bisphosphonate therapy immediately", "A Z-score of -2.4 is normal and requires no further workup because the patient''s bone density is expected for her age", "The Z-score is only used in children under age 10; for all adults, the T-score is the sole diagnostic standard", "In premenopausal women and men under 50, the Z-score (comparison to age- and sex-matched peers) is the preferred metric; a Z-score <= -2.0 is classified as ''below expected range for age'' and should prompt investigation for secondary causes of bone loss"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For **premenopausal women, men under 50, and children**, the International Society for Clinical Densitometry (ISCD) recommends using the **Z-score**, which compares the patient''s BMD to age-, sex-, and ethnicity-matched controls. A **Z-score <= -2.0** is classified as **''below the expected range for age''** and should trigger a thorough workup for **secondary causes of bone loss** (e.g., hyperparathyroidism, hyperthyroidism, celiac disease, multiple myeloma, chronic glucocorticoid use, hypogonadism, vitamin D deficiency). The WHO T-score criteria (osteoporosis <= -2.5) are validated only for postmenopausal women and men >= 50 years. DEXA remains the gold standard for clinical BMD measurement. (Ref: ISCD 2019 Official Positions; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
