    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old premenopausal woman is referred for bone density testing after sustaining a low-trauma wrist fracture. Her DEXA scan reveals a femoral neck T-score of -2.8 and a Z-score of -2.4. Which of the following is the most accurate interpretation and the most appropriate clinical reasoning regarding the choice of reference metric?', '["The Z-score is only used in children under age 10; for all adults, the T-score is the sole diagnostic standard", "Both T-scores and Z-scores are obsolete; quantitative CT (QCT) has replaced DEXA as the gold standard for diagnosing osteoporosis", "In premenopausal women and men under 50, the Z-score (comparison to age- and sex-matched peers) is the preferred metric; a Z-score <= -2.0 is classified as ''below expected range for age'' and should prompt investigation for secondary causes of bone loss", "The T-score of -2.8 confirms osteoporosis regardless of patient age; initiate bisphosphonate therapy immediately", "A Z-score of -2.4 is normal and requires no further workup because the patient''s bone density is expected for her age"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For **premenopausal women, men under 50, and children**, the International Society for Clinical Densitometry (ISCD) recommends using the **Z-score**, which compares the patient''s BMD to age-, sex-, and ethnicity-matched controls. A **Z-score <= -2.0** is classified as **''below the expected range for age''** and should trigger a thorough workup for **secondary causes of bone loss** (e.g., hyperparathyroidism, hyperthyroidism, celiac disease, multiple myeloma, chronic glucocorticoid use, hypogonadism, vitamin D deficiency). The WHO T-score criteria (osteoporosis <= -2.5) are validated only for postmenopausal women and men >= 50 years. DEXA remains the gold standard for clinical BMD measurement. (Ref: ISCD 2019 Official Positions; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old postmenopausal woman undergoes a DEXA scan for osteoporosis screening. The report shows T-scores at the lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, total hip, and distal one-third radius. The T-scores are: lumbar spine -1.8, femoral neck -2.7, total hip -1.8, and distal radius -2.0. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding DEXA site interpretation and the patient''s diagnosis?', '["The diagnosis must be based exclusively on the lumbar spine T-score because it is the most metabolically active site and the only validated location", "The diagnosis is based on the LOWEST T-score among the central skeletal sites (lumbar spine, femoral neck, or total hip); the peripheral forearm is used when central sites are invalid (e.g., bilateral hip replacements, severe degenerative changes)", "DEXA results at different sites cannot be compared; each site must be evaluated independently with site-specific treatment thresholds", "An average of all four sites should be calculated; the mean T-score determines the diagnosis", "The distal radius T-score always takes precedence over central sites because cortical bone loss is the earliest detectable change"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **diagnosis of osteoporosis is based on the lowest T-score** among the standard central skeletal measurement sites: the **lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, and total hip**. If central sites are invalid due to artifacts (e.g., severe degenerative disease, compression fractures, bilateral hip arthroplasties, or morbid obesity), the **distal one-third (33%) radius** of the non-dominant arm is used as a valid alternative. The NOF and ISCD recommend using the lowest T-score from any qualifying site for diagnosis and treatment decisions. Sites should not be averaged. The femoral neck T-score is specifically used for FRAX calculations. (Ref: ISCD 2019; NOF Clinician''s Guide; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old postmenopausal woman undergoes a DEXA scan for osteoporosis screening. The report shows T-scores at the lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, total hip, and distal one-third radius. The T-scores are: lumbar spine -1.5, femoral neck -2.9, total hip -1.8, and distal radius -2.0. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding DEXA site interpretation and the patient''s diagnosis?', '["The distal radius T-score always takes precedence over central sites because cortical bone loss is the earliest detectable change", "DEXA results at different sites cannot be compared; each site must be evaluated independently with site-specific treatment thresholds", "An average of all four sites should be calculated; the mean T-score determines the diagnosis", "The diagnosis is based on the LOWEST T-score among the central skeletal sites (lumbar spine, femoral neck, or total hip); the peripheral forearm is used when central sites are invalid (e.g., bilateral hip replacements, severe degenerative changes)", "The diagnosis must be based exclusively on the lumbar spine T-score because it is the most metabolically active site and the only validated location"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **diagnosis of osteoporosis is based on the lowest T-score** among the standard central skeletal measurement sites: the **lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, and total hip**. If central sites are invalid due to artifacts (e.g., severe degenerative disease, compression fractures, bilateral hip arthroplasties, or morbid obesity), the **distal one-third (33%) radius** of the non-dominant arm is used as a valid alternative. The NOF and ISCD recommend using the lowest T-score from any qualifying site for diagnosis and treatment decisions. Sites should not be averaged. The femoral neck T-score is specifically used for FRAX calculations. (Ref: ISCD 2019; NOF Clinician''s Guide; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
