    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old postmenopausal woman undergoes a DEXA scan for osteoporosis screening. The report shows T-scores at the lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, total hip, and distal one-third radius. The T-scores are: lumbar spine -3.2, femoral neck -2.1, total hip -1.8, and distal radius -2.0. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding DEXA site interpretation and the patient''s diagnosis?', '["An average of all four sites should be calculated; the mean T-score determines the diagnosis", "The diagnosis must be based exclusively on the lumbar spine T-score because it is the most metabolically active site and the only validated location", "The diagnosis is based on the LOWEST T-score among the central skeletal sites (lumbar spine, femoral neck, or total hip); the peripheral forearm is used when central sites are invalid (e.g., bilateral hip replacements, severe degenerative changes)", "DEXA results at different sites cannot be compared; each site must be evaluated independently with site-specific treatment thresholds", "The distal radius T-score always takes precedence over central sites because cortical bone loss is the earliest detectable change"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **diagnosis of osteoporosis is based on the lowest T-score** among the standard central skeletal measurement sites: the **lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, and total hip**. If central sites are invalid due to artifacts (e.g., severe degenerative disease, compression fractures, bilateral hip arthroplasties, or morbid obesity), the **distal one-third (33%) radius** of the non-dominant arm is used as a valid alternative. The NOF and ISCD recommend using the lowest T-score from any qualifying site for diagnosis and treatment decisions. Sites should not be averaged. The femoral neck T-score is specifically used for FRAX calculations. (Ref: ISCD 2019; NOF Clinician''s Guide; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
