    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old postmenopausal female undergoes a DEXA scan for osteoporosis screening. The report shows T-scores at the lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, total hip, and distal one-third radius. The T-scores are: lumbar spine -2.8, femoral neck -2.5, total hip -1.8, and distal radius -2.0. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding DEXA site interpretation and the patient''s diagnosis?', '["The diagnosis is based on the LOWEST T-score among the central skeletal sites (lumbar spine, femoral neck, or total hip); the peripheral forearm is used when central sites are invalid (e.g., bilateral hip replacements, severe degenerative changes)", "An average of all four sites should be calculated; the mean T-score determines the diagnosis", "DEXA results at different sites cannot be compared; each site must be evaluated independently with site-specific treatment thresholds", "The diagnosis must be based exclusively on the lumbar spine T-score because it is the most metabolically active site and the only validated location", "The distal radius T-score always takes precedence over central sites because cortical bone loss is the earliest detectable change"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **diagnosis of osteoporosis is based on the lowest T-score** among the standard central skeletal measurement sites: the **lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, and total hip**. If central sites are invalid due to artifacts (e.g., severe degenerative disease, compression fractures, bilateral hip arthroplasties, or morbid obesity), the **distal one-third (33%) radius** of the non-dominant arm is used as a valid alternative. The NOF and ISCD recommend using the lowest T-score from any qualifying site for diagnosis and treatment decisions. Sites should not be averaged. The femoral neck T-score is specifically used for FRAX calculations. (Ref: ISCD 2019; NOF Clinician''s Guide; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old postmenopausal woman undergoes a DEXA scan for osteoporosis screening. The report shows T-scores at the lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, total hip, and distal one-third radius. The T-scores are: lumbar spine -1.8, femoral neck -2.1, total hip -1.8, and distal radius -2.0. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding DEXA site interpretation and the patient''s diagnosis?', '["The diagnosis is based on the LOWEST T-score among the central skeletal sites (lumbar spine, femoral neck, or total hip); the peripheral forearm is used when central sites are invalid (e.g., bilateral hip replacements, severe degenerative changes)", "An average of all four sites should be calculated; the mean T-score determines the diagnosis", "The diagnosis must be based exclusively on the lumbar spine T-score because it is the most metabolically active site and the only validated location", "The distal radius T-score always takes precedence over central sites because cortical bone loss is the earliest detectable change", "DEXA results at different sites cannot be compared; each site must be evaluated independently with site-specific treatment thresholds"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **diagnosis of osteoporosis is based on the lowest T-score** among the standard central skeletal measurement sites: the **lumbar spine (L1-L4), femoral neck, and total hip**. If central sites are invalid due to artifacts (e.g., severe degenerative disease, compression fractures, bilateral hip arthroplasties, or morbid obesity), the **distal one-third (33%) radius** of the non-dominant arm is used as a valid alternative. The NOF and ISCD recommend using the lowest T-score from any qualifying site for diagnosis and treatment decisions. Sites should not be averaged. The femoral neck T-score is specifically used for FRAX calculations. (Ref: ISCD 2019; NOF Clinician''s Guide; Hazzard''s Ch. 77)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old postmenopausal woman has a DEXA T-score of -2.4 at the femoral neck (osteopenia). She has a history of rheumatoid arthritis and chronic prednisone use (5 mg/day for 3 years). Her FRAX 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture is calculated at 22% and hip fracture at 7%. According to the National Osteoporosis Foundation (NOF) treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate management?', '["The FRAX score exceeds the treatment threshold (>= 20% major osteoporotic or >= 3% hip fracture risk); initiate pharmacologic therapy with a bisphosphonate plus calcium and vitamin D, in addition to lifestyle modifications", "FRAX scores are not validated for postmenopausal women; perform a bone biopsy to guide treatment decisions", "Initiate teriparatide immediately because any FRAX score above 10% requires anabolic therapy", "The FRAX score is below the treatment threshold; lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are sufficient without pharmacotherapy", "Repeat the DEXA scan in 6 months because FRAX cannot be calculated from a single DEXA measurement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **NOF/AACE treatment guidelines** recommend initiating pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and men >= 50 with: (1) a **hip or vertebral fragility fracture**, (2) a **T-score <= -2.5** at the femoral neck, total hip, or lumbar spine, OR (3) a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) **AND a FRAX 10-year probability of >= 20% for major osteoporotic fracture OR >= 3% for hip fracture**. Below these FRAX thresholds, lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are appropriate. FRAX incorporates age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, smoking, and alcohol intake. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old postmenopausal woman has a DEXA T-score of -1.4 at the femoral neck (osteopenia). She has a history of low body weight (52 kg) and excessive alcohol intake (4 drinks/day). Her FRAX 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture is calculated at 12% and hip fracture at 2.0%. According to the National Osteoporosis Foundation (NOF) treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate management?', '["FRAX scores are not validated for use in clinical decision-making and should be disregarded", "Prescribe raloxifene as first-line therapy for all patients with FRAX scores below 20%", "The FRAX score is below the treatment threshold (< 20% major osteoporotic AND < 3% hip fracture risk); lifestyle modifications (calcium 1200 mg/day, vitamin D 800-1000 IU/day, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention, smoking cessation) are appropriate; repeat DEXA in 1-2 years", "Initiate bisphosphonate therapy immediately because any patient with osteopenia requires pharmacotherapy", "Start denosumab because FRAX scores below 20% always indicate the need for biological therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **NOF/AACE treatment guidelines** recommend initiating pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and men >= 50 with: (1) a **hip or vertebral fragility fracture**, (2) a **T-score <= -2.5** at the femoral neck, total hip, or lumbar spine, OR (3) a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) **AND a FRAX 10-year probability of >= 20% for major osteoporotic fracture OR >= 3% for hip fracture**. Below these FRAX thresholds, lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are appropriate. FRAX incorporates age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, smoking, and alcohol intake. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old postmenopausal female has a DEXA T-score of -2.2 at the femoral neck (osteopenia). She has a history of a parental history of hip fracture and current smoking. Her FRAX 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture is calculated at 11% and hip fracture at 1.2%. According to the National Osteoporosis Foundation (NOF) treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate management?', '["FRAX scores are not validated for use in clinical decision-making and should be disregarded", "Start denosumab because FRAX scores below 20% always indicate the need for biological therapy", "The FRAX score is below the treatment threshold (< 20% major osteoporotic AND < 3% hip fracture risk); lifestyle modifications (calcium 1200 mg/day, vitamin D 800-1000 IU/day, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention, smoking cessation) are appropriate; repeat DEXA in 1-2 years", "Prescribe raloxifene as first-line therapy for all patients with FRAX scores below 20%", "Initiate bisphosphonate therapy immediately because any patient with osteopenia requires pharmacotherapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **NOF/AACE treatment guidelines** recommend initiating pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and men >= 50 with: (1) a **hip or vertebral fragility fracture**, (2) a **T-score <= -2.5** at the femoral neck, total hip, or lumbar spine, OR (3) a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) **AND a FRAX 10-year probability of >= 20% for major osteoporotic fracture OR >= 3% for hip fracture**. Below these FRAX thresholds, lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are appropriate. FRAX incorporates age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, smoking, and alcohol intake. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old postmenopausal female has a DEXA T-score of -1.8 at the femoral neck (osteopenia). She has a history of low body weight (52 kg) and excessive alcohol intake (4 drinks/day). Her FRAX 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture is calculated at 34% and hip fracture at 7%. According to the National Osteoporosis Foundation (NOF) treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate management?', '["The FRAX score exceeds the treatment threshold (>= 20% major osteoporotic or >= 3% hip fracture risk); initiate pharmacologic therapy with a bisphosphonate plus calcium and vitamin D, in addition to lifestyle modifications", "Initiate teriparatide immediately because any FRAX score above 10% requires anabolic therapy", "FRAX scores are not validated for postmenopausal women; perform a bone biopsy to guide treatment decisions", "The FRAX score is below the treatment threshold; lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are sufficient without pharmacotherapy", "Repeat the DEXA scan in 6 months because FRAX cannot be calculated from a single DEXA measurement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **NOF/AACE treatment guidelines** recommend initiating pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and men >= 50 with: (1) a **hip or vertebral fragility fracture**, (2) a **T-score <= -2.5** at the femoral neck, total hip, or lumbar spine, OR (3) a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) **AND a FRAX 10-year probability of >= 20% for major osteoporotic fracture OR >= 3% for hip fracture**. Below these FRAX thresholds, lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are appropriate. FRAX incorporates age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, smoking, and alcohol intake. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old postmenopausal woman has a DEXA T-score of -1.2 at the femoral neck (osteopenia). She has a history of a parental history of hip fracture and current smoking. Her FRAX 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture is calculated at 10% and hip fracture at 2.5%. According to the National Osteoporosis Foundation (NOF) treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate management?', '["Start denosumab because FRAX scores below 20% always indicate the need for biological therapy", "FRAX scores are not validated for use in clinical decision-making and should be disregarded", "The FRAX score is below the treatment threshold (< 20% major osteoporotic AND < 3% hip fracture risk); lifestyle modifications (calcium 1200 mg/day, vitamin D 800-1000 IU/day, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention, smoking cessation) are appropriate; repeat DEXA in 1-2 years", "Prescribe raloxifene as first-line therapy for all patients with FRAX scores below 20%", "Initiate bisphosphonate therapy immediately because any patient with osteopenia requires pharmacotherapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **NOF/AACE treatment guidelines** recommend initiating pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and men >= 50 with: (1) a **hip or vertebral fragility fracture**, (2) a **T-score <= -2.5** at the femoral neck, total hip, or lumbar spine, OR (3) a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) **AND a FRAX 10-year probability of >= 20% for major osteoporotic fracture OR >= 3% for hip fracture**. Below these FRAX thresholds, lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are appropriate. FRAX incorporates age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, smoking, and alcohol intake. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old postmenopausal woman has a DEXA T-score of -2.2 at the femoral neck (osteopenia). She has a history of a parental history of hip fracture and current smoking. Her FRAX 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture is calculated at 9% and hip fracture at 1.9%. According to the National Osteoporosis Foundation (NOF) treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate management?', '["The FRAX score is below the treatment threshold (< 20% major osteoporotic AND < 3% hip fracture risk); lifestyle modifications (calcium 1200 mg/day, vitamin D 800-1000 IU/day, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention, smoking cessation) are appropriate; repeat DEXA in 1-2 years", "Initiate bisphosphonate therapy immediately because any patient with osteopenia requires pharmacotherapy", "Prescribe raloxifene as first-line therapy for all patients with FRAX scores below 20%", "FRAX scores are not validated for use in clinical decision-making and should be disregarded", "Start denosumab because FRAX scores below 20% always indicate the need for biological therapy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **NOF/AACE treatment guidelines** recommend initiating pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and men >= 50 with: (1) a **hip or vertebral fragility fracture**, (2) a **T-score <= -2.5** at the femoral neck, total hip, or lumbar spine, OR (3) a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) **AND a FRAX 10-year probability of >= 20% for major osteoporotic fracture OR >= 3% for hip fracture**. Below these FRAX thresholds, lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are appropriate. FRAX incorporates age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, smoking, and alcohol intake. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old postmenopausal female has a DEXA T-score of -1.4 at the femoral neck (osteopenia). She has a history of early menopause at age 42 and a maternal history of hip fracture. Her FRAX 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture is calculated at 31% and hip fracture at 9%. According to the National Osteoporosis Foundation (NOF) treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate management?', '["The FRAX score is below the treatment threshold; lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are sufficient without pharmacotherapy", "Repeat the DEXA scan in 6 months because FRAX cannot be calculated from a single DEXA measurement", "FRAX scores are not validated for postmenopausal women; perform a bone biopsy to guide treatment decisions", "Initiate teriparatide immediately because any FRAX score above 10% requires anabolic therapy", "The FRAX score exceeds the treatment threshold (>= 20% major osteoporotic or >= 3% hip fracture risk); initiate pharmacologic therapy with a bisphosphonate plus calcium and vitamin D, in addition to lifestyle modifications"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **NOF/AACE treatment guidelines** recommend initiating pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and men >= 50 with: (1) a **hip or vertebral fragility fracture**, (2) a **T-score <= -2.5** at the femoral neck, total hip, or lumbar spine, OR (3) a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) **AND a FRAX 10-year probability of >= 20% for major osteoporotic fracture OR >= 3% for hip fracture**. Below these FRAX thresholds, lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are appropriate. FRAX incorporates age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, smoking, and alcohol intake. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
