    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old postmenopausal female has a DEXA T-score of -1.8 at the femoral neck (osteopenia). She has a history of early menopause at age 42 and a maternal history of hip fracture. Her FRAX 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture is calculated at 29% and hip fracture at 4%. According to the National Osteoporosis Foundation (NOF) treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate management?', '["The FRAX score exceeds the treatment threshold (>= 20% major osteoporotic or >= 3% hip fracture risk); initiate pharmacologic therapy with a bisphosphonate plus calcium and vitamin D, in addition to lifestyle modifications", "FRAX scores are not validated for postmenopausal women; perform a bone biopsy to guide treatment decisions", "Repeat the DEXA scan in 6 months because FRAX cannot be calculated from a single DEXA measurement", "Initiate teriparatide immediately because any FRAX score above 10% requires anabolic therapy", "The FRAX score is below the treatment threshold; lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are sufficient without pharmacotherapy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **NOF/AACE treatment guidelines** recommend initiating pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and men >= 50 with: (1) a **hip or vertebral fragility fracture**, (2) a **T-score <= -2.5** at the femoral neck, total hip, or lumbar spine, OR (3) a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) **AND a FRAX 10-year probability of >= 20% for major osteoporotic fracture OR >= 3% for hip fracture**. Below these FRAX thresholds, lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are appropriate. FRAX incorporates age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, smoking, and alcohol intake. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old postmenopausal female has a DEXA T-score of -1.6 at the femoral neck (osteopenia). She has a history of rheumatoid arthritis and chronic prednisone use (5 mg/day for 3 years). Her FRAX 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture is calculated at 8% and hip fracture at 2.1%. According to the National Osteoporosis Foundation (NOF) treatment guidelines, what is the most appropriate management?', '["Prescribe raloxifene as first-line therapy for all patients with FRAX scores below 20%", "Start denosumab because FRAX scores below 20% always indicate the need for biological therapy", "The FRAX score is below the treatment threshold (< 20% major osteoporotic AND < 3% hip fracture risk); lifestyle modifications (calcium 1200 mg/day, vitamin D 800-1000 IU/day, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention, smoking cessation) are appropriate; repeat DEXA in 1-2 years", "FRAX scores are not validated for use in clinical decision-making and should be disregarded", "Initiate bisphosphonate therapy immediately because any patient with osteopenia requires pharmacotherapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **NOF/AACE treatment guidelines** recommend initiating pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and men >= 50 with: (1) a **hip or vertebral fragility fracture**, (2) a **T-score <= -2.5** at the femoral neck, total hip, or lumbar spine, OR (3) a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) **AND a FRAX 10-year probability of >= 20% for major osteoporotic fracture OR >= 3% for hip fracture**. Below these FRAX thresholds, lifestyle modifications (calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention) are appropriate. FRAX incorporates age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, smoking, and alcohol intake. (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide 2014; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old postmenopausal woman was started on alendronate 6 years ago for osteoporosis (initial femoral neck T-score of -2.7). She has been adherent to therapy and takes calcium and vitamin D supplementation. She asks when her next DEXA scan should be performed. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding bone density monitoring during osteoporosis treatment?', '["Repeat DEXA only after completing a full 10-year course of bisphosphonate therapy", "Repeat DEXA every 6 months to detect rapid bone loss and adjust medication doses accordingly", "DEXA monitoring is unnecessary once pharmacotherapy is initiated; treatment efficacy is determined solely by the absence of new fractures", "Repeat DEXA 1-2 years after initiating or changing therapy to assess treatment response; once stable, extend the interval to every 2 years", "Repeat DEXA every 5 years regardless of treatment status, as bone density changes too slowly to detect in shorter intervals"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **ISCD and NOF recommend repeat DEXA 1-2 years after initiating or changing osteoporosis therapy** to assess treatment response. A significant change in BMD is defined as a change exceeding the **least significant change (LSC)**, which is typically 3-6% at the lumbar spine and 4-6% at the hip. Once a stable BMD response is confirmed, the monitoring interval can be extended to **every 2 years**. More frequent scanning (e.g., every 6 months) is not recommended because bone density changes are too small to be detected reliably within that timeframe given the precision error of DEXA. Monitoring is essential to confirm treatment efficacy and adherence, and to detect treatment failure (continued bone loss despite therapy). (Ref: ISCD 2019; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NOF Clinician''s Guide)'),
