    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old postmenopausal female was started on alendronate 7 years ago for osteoporosis (initial femoral neck T-score of -2.7). She has been adherent to therapy and takes calcium and vitamin D supplementation. She asks when her next DEXA scan should be performed. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding bone density monitoring during osteoporosis treatment?', '["DEXA monitoring is unnecessary once pharmacotherapy is initiated; treatment efficacy is determined solely by the absence of new fractures", "Repeat DEXA 1-2 years after initiating or changing therapy to assess treatment response; once stable, extend the interval to every 2 years", "Repeat DEXA only after completing a full 10-year course of bisphosphonate therapy", "Repeat DEXA every 5 years regardless of treatment status, as bone density changes too slowly to detect in shorter intervals", "Repeat DEXA every 6 months to detect rapid bone loss and adjust medication doses accordingly"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **ISCD and NOF recommend repeat DEXA 1-2 years after initiating or changing osteoporosis therapy** to assess treatment response. A significant change in BMD is defined as a change exceeding the **least significant change (LSC)**, which is typically 3-6% at the lumbar spine and 4-6% at the hip. Once a stable BMD response is confirmed, the monitoring interval can be extended to **every 2 years**. More frequent scanning (e.g., every 6 months) is not recommended because bone density changes are too small to be detected reliably within that timeframe given the precision error of DEXA. Monitoring is essential to confirm treatment efficacy and adherence, and to detect treatment failure (continued bone loss despite therapy). (Ref: ISCD 2019; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; NOF Clinician''s Guide)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old postmenopausal woman is diagnosed with osteoporosis (femoral neck T-score -2.9). Alendronate is prescribed as first-line therapy. The patient asks how the medication works. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of bisphosphonates and their effect on bone remodeling?', '["Bisphosphonates increase intestinal calcium absorption by upregulating 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the kidney, thereby increasing active vitamin D levels", "Bisphosphonates act as selective estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs) that mimic estrogen''s protective effect on bone", "Bisphosphonates are potent inhibitors of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption; they bind to hydroxyapatite in bone and are internalized by osteoclasts during resorption, where they inhibit the mevalonate/farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase pathway, inducing osteoclast apoptosis", "Bisphosphonates stimulate osteoblast proliferation and new bone formation without affecting osteoclast activity, making them purely anabolic agents", "Bisphosphonates inhibit RANKL binding to RANK, preventing osteoclast differentiation and activation in the same manner as denosumab"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, zoledronic acid) are **anti-resorptive agents** that are the **first-line pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis**. They have a high affinity for **hydroxyapatite** in mineralized bone. During osteoclast-mediated resorption, bisphosphonates are released from the bone surface and internalized by osteoclasts. Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates (all modern agents) inhibit **farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (FPPS)** in the **mevalonate pathway**, disrupting osteoclast function and inducing **osteoclast apoptosis**. This reduces bone turnover and net bone resorption, increasing BMD over time. They do NOT stimulate osteoblasts (that is the mechanism of teriparatide). They are NOT SERMs (that is raloxifene). They do NOT inhibit RANKL (that is denosumab). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK; Goodman & Gilman''s Pharmacology)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old postmenopausal woman is diagnosed with osteoporosis (femoral neck T-score -2.9). Alendronate is prescribed as first-line therapy. The patient asks how the medication works. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of bisphosphonates and their effect on bone remodeling?', '["Bisphosphonates act as selective estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs) that mimic estrogen''s protective effect on bone", "Bisphosphonates increase intestinal calcium absorption by upregulating 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the kidney, thereby increasing active vitamin D levels", "Bisphosphonates are potent inhibitors of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption; they bind to hydroxyapatite in bone and are internalized by osteoclasts during resorption, where they inhibit the mevalonate/farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase pathway, inducing osteoclast apoptosis", "Bisphosphonates stimulate osteoblast proliferation and new bone formation without affecting osteoclast activity, making them purely anabolic agents", "Bisphosphonates inhibit RANKL binding to RANK, preventing osteoclast differentiation and activation in the same manner as denosumab"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, zoledronic acid) are **anti-resorptive agents** that are the **first-line pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis**. They have a high affinity for **hydroxyapatite** in mineralized bone. During osteoclast-mediated resorption, bisphosphonates are released from the bone surface and internalized by osteoclasts. Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates (all modern agents) inhibit **farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (FPPS)** in the **mevalonate pathway**, disrupting osteoclast function and inducing **osteoclast apoptosis**. This reduces bone turnover and net bone resorption, increasing BMD over time. They do NOT stimulate osteoblasts (that is the mechanism of teriparatide). They are NOT SERMs (that is raloxifene). They do NOT inhibit RANKL (that is denosumab). (Ref: Hazzard''