    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old postmenopausal woman is diagnosed with osteoporosis (femoral neck T-score -2.9). Alendronate is prescribed as first-line therapy. The patient asks how the medication works. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of bisphosphonates and their effect on bone remodeling?', '["Bisphosphonates act as selective estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs) that mimic estrogen''s protective effect on bone", "Bisphosphonates are potent inhibitors of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption; they bind to hydroxyapatite in bone and are internalized by osteoclasts during resorption, where they inhibit the mevalonate/farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase pathway, inducing osteoclast apoptosis", "Bisphosphonates stimulate osteoblast proliferation and new bone formation without affecting osteoclast activity, making them purely anabolic agents", "Bisphosphonates inhibit RANKL binding to RANK, preventing osteoclast differentiation and activation in the same manner as denosumab", "Bisphosphonates increase intestinal calcium absorption by upregulating 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the kidney, thereby increasing active vitamin D levels"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, zoledronic acid) are **anti-resorptive agents** that are the **first-line pharmacotherapy for osteoporosis**. They have a high affinity for **hydroxyapatite** in mineralized bone. During osteoclast-mediated resorption, bisphosphonates are released from the bone surface and internalized by osteoclasts. Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates (all modern agents) inhibit **farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (FPPS)** in the **mevalonate pathway**, disrupting osteoclast function and inducing **osteoclast apoptosis**. This reduces bone turnover and net bone resorption, increasing BMD over time. They do NOT stimulate osteoblasts (that is the mechanism of teriparatide). They are NOT SERMs (that is raloxifene). They do NOT inhibit RANKL (that is denosumab). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK; Goodman & Gilman''s Pharmacology)'),
