    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old postmenopausal female is prescribed oral alendronate 70 mg weekly for newly diagnosed osteoporosis. Which of the following instructions regarding proper administration is most important to ensure adequate drug absorption and minimize the risk of adverse effects?', '["Take the tablet with food to minimize the risk of nausea; lying down immediately after ingestion is acceptable", "Take the tablet with coffee or orange juice because acidic beverages enhance bisphosphonate bioavailability", "Take the tablet at bedtime with a glass of milk to enhance calcium-mediated absorption and reduce gastrointestinal upset", "Take the tablet at any time of day with any beverage; the timing and food intake do not affect absorption or safety", "Take the tablet first thing in the morning on an empty stomach with a full glass (6-8 oz) of plain water; remain upright (sitting or standing) for at least 30 minutes before eating, drinking anything else, or taking other medications, to prevent esophageal irritation and ensure absorption"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Oral bisphosphonates have extremely **low bioavailability (< 1%)**. To maximize absorption and minimize esophageal adverse effects, patients must: (1) Take the tablet **first thing in the morning on an empty stomach** (fasting), (2) Swallow with a **full glass (6-8 oz) of plain water only** (no coffee, juice, milk, or mineral water, as calcium, magnesium, iron, and other minerals chelate bisphosphonates and prevent absorption), (3) **Remain upright (sitting or standing) for at least 30 minutes** (60 minutes for ibandronate) to prevent esophageal irritation, ulceration, and stricture, and (4) **Wait at least 30 minutes before eating, drinking, or taking other medications**. Non-adherence to these instructions is the most common cause of treatment failure and esophageal complications. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; First Aid Step 2 CK; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old woman on weekly oral alendronate for 4 months presents with progressive dysphagia, retrosternal burning, and odynophagia. Upper endoscopy reveals discrete esophageal ulcerations in the distal esophagus. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?', '["Reduce the alendronate dose to 35 mg weekly and instruct the patient to take it with food to coat the esophagus", "Discontinue all osteoporosis therapy permanently, as the esophageal injury indicates a contraindication to all anti-resorptive agents", "Switch to oral risedronate, which has no risk of esophageal injury unlike alendronate", "Discontinue oral alendronate immediately; switch to an intravenous bisphosphonate (e.g., annual zoledronic acid) or a non-bisphosphonate agent (e.g., denosumab) to avoid further esophageal injury while continuing osteoporosis treatment", "Continue oral alendronate but add a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) to heal the esophageal ulcers while maintaining therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Esophageal ulceration, erosion, and stricture are well-recognized adverse effects of **oral bisphosphonates**, particularly when administration instructions are not followed (e.g., lying down after ingestion, taking with insufficient water, or taking at bedtime). When significant esophageal injury occurs, **oral bisphosphonates must be discontinued** and the patient should be switched to an **intravenous bisphosphonate** (e.g., zoledronic acid 5 mg IV annually or ibandronate 3 mg IV quarterly) or a **non-bisphosphonate agent** (e.g., denosumab). Simply adding a PPI does not address the direct caustic injury mechanism. Oral bisphosphonates are **contraindicated** in patients with esophageal disorders that delay emptying (e.g., achalasia, strictures) or inability to remain upright for 30 minutes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; FDA Safety Communication; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female on weekly oral alendronate for 4 months presents with progressive dysphagia, retrosternal burning, and odynophagia. Upper endoscopy reveals discrete esophageal ulcerations in the distal esophagus. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?', '["Continue oral alendronate but add a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) to heal the esophageal ulcers while maintaining therapy", "Switch to oral risedronate, which has no risk of esophageal injury unlike alendronate", "Reduce the alendronate dose to 35 mg weekly and instruct the patient to take it with food to coat the esophagus", "Discontinue all osteoporosis therapy permanently, as the esophageal injury indicates a contraindication to all anti-resorptive agents", "Discontinue oral alendronate immediately; switch to an intravenous bisphosphonate (e.g., annual zoledronic acid) or a non-bisphosphonate agent (e.g., denosumab) to avoid further esophageal injury while continuing osteoporosis treatment"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Esophageal ulceration, erosion, and stricture are well-recognized adverse effects of **oral bisphosphonates**, particularly when administration instructions are not followed (e.g., lying down after ingestion, taking with insufficient water, or taking at bedtime). When significant esophageal injury occurs, **oral bisphosphonates must be discontinued** and the patient should be switched to an **intravenous bisphosphonate** (e.g., zoledronic acid 5 mg IV annually or ibandronate 3 mg IV quarterly) or a **non-bisphosphonate agent** (e.g., denosumab). Simply adding a PPI does not address the direct caustic injury mechanism. Oral bisphosphonates are **contraindicated** in patients with esophageal disorders that delay emptying (e.g., achalasia, strictures) or inability to remain upright for 30 minutes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; FDA Safety Communication; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
