    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old woman on weekly oral alendronate for 3 months presents with progressive dysphagia, retrosternal burning, and odynophagia. Upper endoscopy reveals discrete esophageal ulcerations in the distal esophagus. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?', '["Reduce the alendronate dose to 35 mg weekly and instruct the patient to take it with food to coat the esophagus", "Discontinue oral alendronate immediately; switch to an intravenous bisphosphonate (e.g., annual zoledronic acid) or a non-bisphosphonate agent (e.g., denosumab) to avoid further esophageal injury while continuing osteoporosis treatment", "Continue oral alendronate but add a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) to heal the esophageal ulcers while maintaining therapy", "Switch to oral risedronate, which has no risk of esophageal injury unlike alendronate", "Discontinue all osteoporosis therapy permanently, as the esophageal injury indicates a contraindication to all anti-resorptive agents"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Esophageal ulceration, erosion, and stricture are well-recognized adverse effects of **oral bisphosphonates**, particularly when administration instructions are not followed (e.g., lying down after ingestion, taking with insufficient water, or taking at bedtime). When significant esophageal injury occurs, **oral bisphosphonates must be discontinued** and the patient should be switched to an **intravenous bisphosphonate** (e.g., zoledronic acid 5 mg IV annually or ibandronate 3 mg IV quarterly) or a **non-bisphosphonate agent** (e.g., denosumab). Simply adding a PPI does not address the direct caustic injury mechanism. Oral bisphosphonates are **contraindicated** in patients with esophageal disorders that delay emptying (e.g., achalasia, strictures) or inability to remain upright for 30 minutes. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; FDA Safety Communication; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old woman who has been receiving oral risedronate for osteoporosis for 6 years presents with exposed, necrotic bone in the mandible that has persisted for 14 weeks despite conservative wound care. She recently underwent a molar extraction. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding bisphosphonate-related osteonecrosis of the jaw (BRONJ)?', '["BRONJ is an extremely common complication affecting > 25% of all patients on oral bisphosphonates for osteoporosis", "BRONJ only occurs in the mandible and never affects the maxilla", "BRONJ occurs exclusively with oral bisphosphonates and never with intravenous formulations", "BRONJ is defined as exposed bone in the maxillofacial region persisting for > 8 weeks in a patient on anti-resorptive or anti-angiogenic therapy with no history of radiation; risk is higher with IV bisphosphonates used in oncology doses than with oral osteoporosis doses", "A dental extraction performed during bisphosphonate therapy always leads to BRONJ, so all teeth should be extracted prophylactically before starting therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Bisphosphonate-related osteonecrosis of the jaw (BRONJ)** is defined by the AAOMS as **exposed bone in the maxillofacial region that has persisted for > 8 weeks** in a patient currently or previously treated with anti-resorptive or anti-angiogenic agents, **with no history of radiation therapy to the jaw**. The risk is significantly **higher with high-dose IV bisphosphonates** used in oncology (e.g., zoledronic acid for bone metastases) than with standard oral doses for osteoporosis. The incidence in osteoporosis patients is very low (~1/10,000 to 1/100,000 patient-years). **Invasive dental procedures** (extractions, implant placement) are the most common precipitating events. Prevention includes completing necessary dental work **before** initiating bisphosphonate therapy and maintaining good oral hygiene. Both the mandible and maxilla can be affected. (Ref: AAOMS 2014 Position Paper; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female who has been receiving denosumab for osteoporosis for 9 years presents with exposed, necrotic bone in the maxilla that has persisted for 10 weeks despite conservative wound care. She recently underwent a tooth extraction. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding bisphosphonate-related osteonecrosis of the jaw (BRONJ)?', '["BRONJ is defined as exposed bone in the maxillofacial region persisting for > 8 weeks in a patient on anti-resorptive or anti-angiogenic therapy with no history of radiation; risk is higher with IV bisphosphonates used in oncology doses than with oral osteoporosis doses", "BRONJ only occurs in the mandible and never affects the maxilla", "BRONJ occurs exclusively with oral bisphosphonates and never with intravenous formulations", "A dental extraction performed during bisphosphonate therapy always leads to BRONJ, so all teeth should be extracted prophylactically before starting therapy", "BRONJ is an extremely common complication affecting > 25% of all patients on oral bisphosphonates for osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Bisphosphonate-related osteonecrosis of the jaw (BRONJ)** is defined by the AAOMS as **exposed bone in the maxillofacial region that has persisted for > 8 weeks** in a patient currently or previously treated with anti-resorptive or anti-angiogenic agents, **with no history of radiation therapy to the jaw**. The risk is significantly **higher with high-dose IV bisphosphonates** used in oncology (e.g., zoledronic acid for bone metastases) than with standard oral doses for osteoporosis. The incidence in osteoporosis patients is very low (~1/10,000 to 1/100,000 patient-years). **Invasive dental procedures** (extractions, implant placement) are the most common precipitating events. Prevention includes completing necessary dental work **before** initiating bisphosphonate therapy and maintaining good oral hygiene. Both the mandible and maxilla can be affected. (Ref: AAOMS 2014 Position Paper; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Current Geriatrics)'),
