    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female who has been receiving oral alendronate for osteoporosis for 4 years presents with exposed, necrotic bone in the maxilla that has persisted for 12 weeks despite conservative wound care. She recently underwent a dental implant placement. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding bisphosphonate-related osteonecrosis of the jaw (BRONJ)?', '["A dental extraction performed during bisphosphonate therapy always leads to BRONJ, so all teeth should be extracted prophylactically before starting therapy", "BRONJ only occurs in the mandible and never affects the maxilla", "BRONJ occurs exclusively with oral bisphosphonates and never with intravenous formulations", "BRONJ is defined as exposed bone in the maxillofacial region persisting for > 8 weeks in a patient on anti-resorptive or anti-angiogenic therapy with no history of radiation; risk is higher with IV bisphosphonates used in oncology doses than with oral osteoporosis doses", "BRONJ is an extremely common complication affecting > 25% of all patients on oral bisphosphonates for osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Bisphosphonate-related osteonecrosis of the jaw (BRONJ)** is defined by the AAOMS as **exposed bone in the maxillofacial region that has persisted for > 8 weeks** in a patient currently or previously treated with anti-resorptive or anti-angiogenic agents, **with no history of radiation therapy to the jaw**. The risk is significantly **higher with high-dose IV bisphosphonates** used in oncology (e.g., zoledronic acid for bone metastases) than with standard oral doses for osteoporosis. The incidence in osteoporosis patients is very low (~1/10,000 to 1/100,000 patient-years). **Invasive dental procedures** (extractions, implant placement) are the most common precipitating events. Prevention includes completing necessary dental work **before** initiating bisphosphonate therapy and maintaining good oral hygiene. Both the mandible and maxilla can be affected. (Ref: